## EESSI

[EESSI](https://www.eessi.io/), the European Environment for Scientific
Software Installations, is a shared stack of optimized scientific software that
is distributed through [CernVM-FS](https://cernvm.cern.ch/fs/). CernVM-FS is a
read-only, HTTP-based network file system: clients mount a repository on demand
under `/cvmfs` and download only the files that are actually accessed into a
local cache. This optional section installs the CernVM-FS client on the *head
node* and in the compute image, makes the `software.eessi.io` repository
available, and adds a module that gives users access to the EESSI software
through the usual `module` commands.

::: {.tip}
The configuration shown here lets every node fetch content directly from the
public EESSI servers, which is adequate for a small test cluster. For a
production cluster it is highly recommended to run a local [Squid
proxy](https://cvmfs-contrib.github.io/cvmfs-tutorial-2021/03_stratum1_proxies/)
and point the clients at it with `CVMFS_HTTP_PROXY` in
`/etc/cvmfs/default.local`, so that repeated downloads are served from inside
the cluster and the load on the public servers is reduced. See the EESSI
documentation on [native installation on
clusters](https://www.eessi.io/docs/getting_access/native_installation/#native-install-on-clusters)
for details.
:::

The CernVM-FS client requires a local cache. On the *head node* the cache is
kept on disk in the default location (`/var/lib/cvmfs`) with a limit of 10 GB:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_eessi -->
```bash
# Install CernVM-FS on the head node
{{ pkg_install }} \
	https://cvmrepo.s3.cern.ch/cvmrepo/yum/cvmfs-release-latest.noarch.rpm
{{ pkg_install }} cvmfs

# Install the EESSI configuration for CernVM-FS
eessi_rpm="https://github.com/EESSI/filesystem-layer/releases/download"
eessi_rpm="${eessi_rpm}/latest/cvmfs-config-eessi-latest.noarch.rpm"
{{ pkg_install }} "${eessi_rpm}"

# Create the client configuration (no proxy, 10 GB local cache)
echo 'CVMFS_CLIENT_PROFILE="single"' > /etc/cvmfs/default.local
echo 'CVMFS_QUOTA_LIMIT=10000' >> /etc/cvmfs/default.local

# Configure autofs and make the EESSI repository accessible
cvmfs_config setup
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

The compute nodes boot a stateless image and have no local disk for the cache,
so the cache is placed in memory under `/dev/shm` and limited to 4 GB. Note
that this memory is no longer available to jobs, so size `CVMFS_QUOTA_LIMIT`
according to the amount of RAM in the compute nodes and the software that is
expected to be used. Alternatives, such as a loopback file per node on a shared
file system, are described in the EESSI documentation on [diskless worker
nodes](https://www.eessi.io/docs/training-events/2025/tutorial-best-practices-cvmfs-hpc/configuration_hpc/#diskless-worker-nodes).
The `cvmfs` package depends on `selinux-policy`, which the Warewulf node
image deliberately does not contain. Installing it brings back
`/etc/selinux/config` with SELinux set to enforcing, and the stateless nodes
would then boot with a policy loaded on an unlabeled root file system and
fail. The configuration is therefore set back to disabled. Because the image
is not booted while it is being customized, `cvmfs_config setup` is told not
to start autofs; the service is enabled instead and starts when the nodes
boot.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_eessi -->
```bash
# Install CernVM-FS and the EESSI configuration in the compute image
{{ pkg_install_chroot }} \
	https://cvmrepo.s3.cern.ch/cvmrepo/yum/cvmfs-release-latest.noarch.rpm
{{ compute_install("cvmfs") }}
{{ compute_install('"${eessi_rpm}"') }}

# cvmfs depends on selinux-policy, which the compute image deliberately
# omits; keep SELinux disabled so the nodes do not boot enforcing
{{ compute_sed("s/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=disabled/", "/etc/selinux/config") }}

# Create the client configuration (no proxy, 4 GB cache in memory)
{{ compute_echo('CVMFS_CLIENT_PROFILE="single"', "/etc/cvmfs/default.local", false) }}
{{ compute_echo("CVMFS_CACHE_BASE=/dev/shm/cvmfs-cache", "/etc/cvmfs/default.local") }}
{{ compute_echo("CVMFS_QUOTA_LIMIT=4000", "/etc/cvmfs/default.local") }}

# Configure autofs in the image and enable it for the next boot
{{ compute_run("cvmfs_config setup nostart") }}
{{ compute_service_enable("autofs") }}
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Finally, add an `eessi` module to the OpenHPC module hierarchy. It appends the
EESSI module directory from the repository to the module search path. Since
`/opt/ohpc/pub` is exported to the compute nodes, the module is available there
as well.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_eessi -->
```bash
# Add an eessi module that extends MODULEPATH with the EESSI modules
cat << 'EOF' > /opt/ohpc/pub/modulefiles/eessi.lua
help([[
Adds the modules of EESSI, the European Environment for Scientific Software
Installations, to the module search path. EESSI is provided through CernVM-FS
under /cvmfs/software.eessi.io. Load one of the EESSI/<version> modules
afterwards to access the software of that EESSI version.
]])
whatis("Name: eessi")
whatis("Description: EESSI software stack provided through CernVM-FS")
whatis("URL: https://www.eessi.io/")

append_path("MODULEPATH", "/cvmfs/software.eessi.io/init/modules")
EOF
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

With the `eessi` module loaded, the `EESSI` module becomes visible. EESSI
publishes one `EESSI/<version>` module per release of its software stack, and
loading one of them provides access to all of the software of that version,
detecting the CPU of the node to select the matching optimized builds. For
example:

```console
[test@sms ~]$ module load eessi
[test@sms ~]$ module avail EESSI

-------------------- /cvmfs/software.eessi.io/init/modules ---------------------
   EESSI/2023.06 (D)    EESSI/2025.06    EESSI/2026.06

  Where:
   D:  Default Module

[test@sms ~]$ module load EESSI/2026.06
[test@sms ~]$ module avail
```
